Chapter: WebSocket
  cites Encode chapter Base64
  cites Foreword chapter Sha1
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 WebSocket protocol (RFC 6455). Frame encoder/decoder for text and
 binary messages. Includes handshake header generation with
 Sec-WebSocket-Accept computation.

 Three things here were wrong in a way no test in this tree could see,
 because nothing outside this chapter ever called it. All three are
 about talking to a peer that is not us.

 The accept key is SHA-1, named by number in the specification. It was
 SHA-256, and the digest was handed to a byte encoder as the word list
 every hash here answers with, so the published example produced a run
 of padding characters instead of an accept key. No browser would have
 completed a handshake.

 A frame sent by a client is required to be masked, and the decoder did
 not unmask, so every message arriving from a browser decoded to the
 four-byte-keyed ciphertext rather than the payload.

 The response was joined with a bare newline. A carriage return has no
 code point in this encoding, so a conforming response cannot be built
 as Text at all; it is built as bytes, which is what a socket takes.

 We say:

Section: Constants
  ws-opcode-text : Integer = 1
  ws-opcode-binary : Integer = 2
  ws-opcode-close : Integer = 8
  ws-opcode-ping : Integer = 9
  ws-opcode-pong : Integer = 10

  ws-guid : Text = "258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11"

Section: Types
  WsFrame = record {
    ws-fin : Boolean,
    ws-opcode : Integer,
    ws-masked : Boolean,
    ws-payload : List Integer,
    ws-length : Integer
  }

Section: Frame Encoding

 A masked frame needs a key, and the frame builder used to take a
 Boolean instead. Passing True set the mask bit over an unmasked
 payload with no key behind it, which is a frame no peer can read.
 The builder now emits what a server sends, and the masked builder
 beside it takes the four key bytes it cannot work without.

  ws-encode-text : Text -> List Integer
  ws-encode-text (msg) =
    let payload = ws-text-to-bytes msg
    in ws-encode-frame True ws-opcode-text payload

  ws-encode-binary : List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-binary (payload) =
    ws-encode-frame True ws-opcode-binary payload

  ws-encode-close : Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-close (code) =
    ws-encode-frame True ws-opcode-close (ws-be16 code)

  ws-encode-ping : List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-ping (payload) =
    ws-encode-frame True ws-opcode-ping payload

  ws-encode-pong : List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-pong (payload) =
    ws-encode-frame True ws-opcode-pong payload

  ws-encode-frame : Boolean, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-frame (fin) (opcode) (payload) =
    let len = list-length payload
    in let byte0 = bit-or (if fin then 128 else 0) opcode
    in if len < 126 then [byte0, len] & payload
    else if len < 65536 then [byte0, 126] & ws-be16 len & payload
    else [byte0, 127] & ws-be64 len & payload

  ws-encode-frame-masked : Boolean, Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-frame-masked (fin) (opcode) (key) (payload) =
    let len = list-length payload
    in let byte0 = bit-or (if fin then 128 else 0) opcode
    in let body = key & ws-apply-mask payload key 0 []
    in if len < 126 then [byte0, bit-or 128 len] & body
    else if len < 65536 then [byte0, bit-or 128 126] & ws-be16 len & body
    else [byte0, bit-or 128 127] & ws-be64 len & body

  ws-encode-text-masked : Text, List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-encode-text-masked (msg) (key) =
    ws-encode-frame-masked True ws-opcode-text key (ws-text-to-bytes msg)

Section: Frame Decoding
  ws-decode-frame : List Integer -> WsFrame
  ws-decode-frame (bytes) =
    let byte0 = list-at bytes 0
    in let byte1 = list-at bytes 1
    in let fin = bit-and byte0 128 > 0
    in let opcode = bit-and byte0 15
    in let masked = bit-and byte1 128 > 0
    in let len-code = bit-and byte1 127
    in let header-end = ws-header-size len-code masked
    in let payload-len = ws-read-length bytes len-code
    in let raw = ws-slice bytes header-end payload-len []
    in let key = ws-slice bytes (ws-header-size len-code False) 4 []
    in let payload = if masked then ws-apply-mask raw key 0 [] else raw
    in WsFrame { ws-fin = fin, ws-opcode = opcode, ws-masked = masked, ws-payload = payload, ws-length = payload-len }

 Masking is a repeating four-byte exclusive-or, so it is its own
 inverse and one function serves the encoder and the decoder both.
 The key sits directly after the length, which is where the header
 would otherwise have ended.

  ws-apply-mask : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-apply-mask (payload) (key) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length payload then acc
    else let k = list-at key (int-mod i 4)
    in ws-apply-mask payload key (i + 1) (list-push acc (bit-xor (list-at payload i) k))

  ws-header-size : Integer, Boolean -> Integer
  ws-header-size (len-code) (masked) =
    let base = if len-code < 126 then 2 else if len-code == 126 then 4 else 10
    in if masked then base + 4 else base

  ws-read-length : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ws-read-length (bytes) (len-code) =
    if len-code < 126 then len-code
    else if len-code == 126 then bit-or (bit-shl (list-at bytes 2) 8) (list-at bytes 3)
    else bit-or (bit-shl (list-at bytes 6) 24) (bit-or (bit-shl (list-at bytes 7) 16) (bit-or (bit-shl (list-at bytes 8) 8) (list-at bytes 9)))

Section: Handshake

 The accept key is the base64 of the SHA-1 of the client key joined to
 the fixed identifier, and the specification prints a worked example
 of it. That example is the test.

  ws-accept-key : Text -> Text
  ws-accept-key (client-key) =
    let combined = client-key & ws-guid
    in base64-encode (sha1-bytes (ws-text-to-bytes combined))

 The response is bytes rather than Text because its line ending is a
 carriage return and a line feed, and a carriage return is one of the
 characters this encoding does not carry. Building it as Text and
 settling for a bare line feed is what the previous version did.

  ws-crlf : List Integer = [13, 10]

  ws-handshake-response-bytes : Text -> List Integer
  ws-handshake-response-bytes (client-key) =
    let l1 = ws-text-to-bytes "HTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols" & ws-crlf
    in let l2 = ws-text-to-bytes "Upgrade: websocket" & ws-crlf
    in let l3 = ws-text-to-bytes "Connection: Upgrade" & ws-crlf
    in let l4 = ws-text-to-bytes ("Sec-WebSocket-Accept: " & ws-accept-key client-key) & ws-crlf
    in l1 & l2 & l3 & l4 & ws-crlf

Section: Helpers
  ws-be16 : Integer -> List Integer
  ws-be16 (v) = [bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  ws-be64 : Integer -> List Integer
  ws-be64 (v) = [0, 0, 0, 0, bit-and (bit-shru v 24) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 16) 255, bit-and (bit-shru v 8) 255, bit-and v 255]

  ws-text-to-bytes : Text -> List Integer
  ws-text-to-bytes (s) = ws-t2b-loop s 0 (text-length s) []

  ws-t2b-loop : Text, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-t2b-loop (s) (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else ws-t2b-loop s (i + 1) len (list-push acc (to-unicode (char-code (char-at s i))))

  ws-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ws-slice (data) (start) (len) (acc) =
    if len <= 0 then acc
    else if start >= list-length data then acc
    else ws-slice data (start + 1) (len - 1) (list-push acc (list-at data start))

Section: Queries
  ws-is-text : WsFrame -> Boolean
  ws-is-text (f) = f.ws-opcode == ws-opcode-text

  ws-is-binary : WsFrame -> Boolean
  ws-is-binary (f) = f.ws-opcode == ws-opcode-binary

  ws-is-close : WsFrame -> Boolean
  ws-is-close (f) = f.ws-opcode == ws-opcode-close

  ws-is-ping : WsFrame -> Boolean
  ws-is-ping (f) = f.ws-opcode == ws-opcode-ping

  format-ws-frame : WsFrame -> Text
  format-ws-frame (f) =
    let op = if f.ws-opcode == 1 then "TEXT" else if f.ws-opcode == 2 then "BIN" else if f.ws-opcode == 8 then "CLOSE" else if f.ws-opcode == 9 then "PING" else "OP-" & show (f.ws-opcode)
    in op & " len=" & show (f.ws-length) & " fin=" & show (f.ws-fin)

